<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <!--
    Nop.Plugin.ExternalAuth.Facebook - task 11.1
    (Requirements 5.5, 1.1-1.5, 2.4, 4.2, 4.3, 5.1, 5.3, 3.1).

    THE RECIPE THIS FOLLOWS is the annotated
    src/Plugins/Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles/Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles.csproj
    (task 10.1) - read that file for the reasoning behind the four silently-failing decisions
    below and the fifth (Content Include vs Update for .txt/.jpg). Only what is DIFFERENT about
    this plugin is documented here.

    ===================================================================================
    WHAT IS DIFFERENT (1) - THIS PLUGIN SHIPS STATIC ASSETS, AND THEY WOULD HAVE 404'd
    ===================================================================================
    Content/facebookstyles.css and Content/Images/facebook-signing.png are referenced by
    ORDINARY URL from Views/PublicInfo.cshtml:

        Html.AddCssFileParts("~/Plugins/ExternalAuth.Facebook/Content/facebookstyles.css");

    and the css in turn names ../Content/Images/facebook-signing.png as the button background.
    They are therefore deployed as loose files (CopyToOutputDirectory="Always", as in 3.90) -
    unlike the .cshtml files, which are compiled into the assembly and NOT deployed.

    That deployment is only half of it. Task 7.4 replaced System.Web's serve-the-whole-app-root
    static handler with an ALLOW-LISTED IFileProvider (Nop.Web/Infrastructure/
    NopStaticFileProvider), and `Plugins/` was not in the allow-list - runtime deferral 10.x-1,
    opened by task 10.x and owned by this task. Task 11.1 added a THIRD-level
    Plugins/<ShortName>/{Content,Scripts}/** rule to it. Without that the stylesheet 404s, and
    because the button's entire appearance comes from the stylesheet the result is an invisible
    control, not a differently-styled one. Asserted over real HTTP by
    Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginStaticAssetTests.

    ===================================================================================
    WHAT IS DIFFERENT (2) - THE ONE PLUGIN WITH A DEAD THIRD-PARTY DEPENDENCY
    ===================================================================================
    The legacy project referenced DotNetOpenAuth 4.3.4 in six assemblies:
    DotNetOpenAuth.AspNet, .Core, .OAuth, .OAuth.Consumer, .OpenId and
    .OpenId.RelyingParty. There is no net10.0 build of any of them, no successor package, and
    the project is unmaintained. They are DELETED and the OAuth 2 authorization-code flow is
    implemented in Core/FacebookProviderAuthorizer.cs against HttpClient + System.Text.Json -
    about 60 lines, half of which 3.90 had already hand-copied out of DotNetOpenAuth's own
    FacebookClient. See the long remarks in that file for the flow, the two behavioural
    differences and the one 3.90 defect that had to be fixed for the flow to work at all.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Facebook was evaluated and rejected; the reasoning,
    including a correction to the instruction that it is "in the shared framework" (it is NOT -
    only Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OAuth is), is in runtime-deferrals.md section 91.1.

    Newtonsoft.Json was a direct <Reference Private=False> here, used only for one
    JObject.Parse of the Graph API's /me response. The rewritten authorizer uses
    System.Text.Json, which is in the shared framework, so this plugin ends up with NO
    PackageReference at all.

    ===================================================================================
    DROPPED IN THE CLASSIC -> SDK CONVERSION (Requirements 1.4, 1.5)
    ===================================================================================
    As in the 10.1 template: ToolsVersion/DefaultTargets/xmlns, ProjectGuid
    {446FED73-BCA1-4D8F-BC13-EA1507C7EF29}, ProductVersion, SchemaVersion, FileAlignment,
    AppDesignerFolder, OutputType, TargetFrameworkProfile, NuGetPackageImportStamp,
    TargetFrameworkVersion v4.5.1, Prefer32Bit, ErrorReport, WarningLevel, DefineConstants and
    the Debug/Release DebugType/DebugSymbols/Optimize blocks; the explicit <Compile Include>
    list of 12 files; the Microsoft.CSharp.targets import, the Microsoft.Bcl.Build.targets
    import and its EnsureNuGetPackageBuildImports guard, and the commented
    BeforeBuild/AfterBuild stubs.

    Every <Reference> with a HintPath into ..\..\packages\ is gone: the Microsoft.AspNet.Mvc
    5.2.3 / Razor 3.2.3 / WebPages 3.2.3 System.Web MVC stack (superseded wholesale by the
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference), Autofac and Newtonsoft.Json (now transitive
    through the project references), the six DotNetOpenAuth assemblies (see above), and the
    net40/net45 polyfills Microsoft.Bcl.Async (Microsoft.Threading.Tasks*), Microsoft.Net.Http
    (System.Net.Http.Extensions/.Primitives) and Microsoft.Web.Infrastructure. The BCL
    <Reference>s (System, System.Configuration, System.Core, System.Net, System.Net.Http,
    System.Net.Http.WebRequest, System.Xml.Linq, System.Data(.DataSetExtensions),
    Microsoft.CSharp, System.Xml) are subsumed by the net10.0 shared framework, and
    <Reference Include="System.Web" /> has no net10.0 counterpart at all.

    app.config, packages.config and web.config are DELETED for the reasons the 10.1 template
    records; web.config's one piece of load-bearing content is transcribed to
    Views/_ViewImports.cshtml. Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs is KEPT (GenerateAssemblyInfo=false
    is solution-wide, so deleting it would drop AssemblyVersion to 0.0.0.0). Notes.txt stays
    non-deployed Content, as in 3.90.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AddRazorSupportForMvc>true</AddRazorSupportForMvc>
    <StaticWebAssetsEnabled>false</StaticWebAssetsEnabled>
    <OutputPath>..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Plugins\ExternalAuth.Facebook\</OutputPath>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The ASP.NET Core shared framework. Required explicitly: Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor with
    AddRazorSupportForMvc does NOT add it implicitly (unlike Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web, where
    restating it fails with NETSDK1086). The controller binds Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc, the
    route provider binds Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing/.Builder, the authorizer binds
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http (IHttpContextAccessor) and the plugin class binds
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing (RouteValueDictionary, on IExternalAuthenticationMethod).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Core\Nop.Core.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Services\Nop.Services.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web.Framework\Nop.Web.Framework.csproj" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Private="false" alone is NOT sufficient - a transitive Nop.Data.dll leaks in through the
    ProjectReference closure and PluginManager would shadow-copy and load it as the process's
    Nop.Data (task 10.1, runtime-deferrals.md section 83.3). Verbatim from the template; the
    "Nop." prefix filter is what keeps Payments.PayPalDirect's private PayPal SDK working
    (task 12.3).
  -->
  <Target Name="NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies" AfterTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ReferenceCopyLocalPaths
          Remove="@(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths)"
          Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Filename)').StartsWith('Nop.'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    View resolution. %(Link) becomes the compiled Razor identifier, so the views compile as
    /Plugins/ExternalAuth.Facebook/Views/*.cshtml and the controller's two
    View("~/Plugins/ExternalAuth.Facebook/Views/....cshtml") call sites stay UNEDITED.
    %(RecursiveDir) already contains "Views/" - do not repeat it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Update="Views\**\*.cshtml"
             Link="Plugins\ExternalAuth.Facebook\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Runtime-read content, deployed exactly as in 3.90.

    Include, NOT Update - AND the <None Remove> first. Under Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor the default
    Content glob covers only .cshtml/.razor, so .txt/.jpg/.css/.png arrive as <None> and a bare
    <Content Update="Description.txt"> would match NOTHING, deploying the plugin without the one
    file PluginManager needs to see it at all.

      Description.txt   parsed by PluginManager.Initialize; its SupportedVersions must contain
                        NopVersion.CurrentVersion ("3.90") - asserted, not assumed, by
                        Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests.
      logo.jpg          shown by the admin plugin list.
      Notes.txt         3.90 declared it Content with no CopyToOutputDirectory, i.e. not deployed.
      Content\**        the static asset tree - see "WHAT IS DIFFERENT (1)" above. A recursive
                        glob rather than 3.90's two explicit entries so an added asset cannot be
                        silently left undeployed; it currently matches exactly those two files.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Remove="Description.txt" />
    <None Remove="logo.jpg" />
    <None Remove="Notes.txt" />
    <None Remove="Content\**" />
    <Content Include="Description.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="logo.jpg" CopyToOutputDirectory="Always" />
    <Content Include="Notes.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
    <Content Include="Content\**" CopyToOutputDirectory="Always" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
